OL,3,ATreasury will not commit to reducing
OL,4,Awelfare bill before next election
OL,5,
OL,6,FThe Treasury is disputing that
OL,7,FChancellor John Healey suggested to the
OL,8,FBBC that the benefits bill will be cut
OL,9,Fbefore the next general election.
OL,11,FWhen Healey was asked by the Today
OL,12,FProgramme on Radio 4 whether there
OL,13,Fwould be a cut in the bill before
OL,14,Fpolling day, he said: "There will be,
OL,15,Fthere will be a reduction in the
OL,16,Fbenefits bill, we'll do that in a
OL,17,FLabour way."
OL,23,APub: 29-OctQppppppppppppppppppppppG1/2
OL,24,ANext PageB Nat NewsC Nat TVF Clean Feed
FL,304,300,200,140,0,100
PN,30302
SC,0002
CT,3,C
PS,c008
OL,0,        ECIMSBWebfax 1   F303A1793271383
OL,1,W5j 5Q7kjs15j 5ws
OL,2,W//%*// //%Q%**,$-.,%,. (,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,
OL,3,ATreasury will not commit to reducing
OL,4,Awelfare bill before next election
OL,5,
OL,6,FBut several Treasury sources took issue
OL,7,Fwith this being interpreted as a
OL,8,Fpromise to do so by the next election,
OL,9,Fwith one suggesting Healey was
OL,10,Fexplicitly attempting to avoid that
OL,11,Fimpression being left.
OL,13,FIt suggests, instead, that ministers
OL,14,Fare not committed to cutting the
OL,15,Fbenefits bill by the next election.
OL,23,APub: 29-OctQppppppppppppppppppppppG2/2
